INFO:agent.loop:\u26a1 Executing tool: 'knowledge_agent' with arguments: {"source_title": "DOJ indictment and Senate/technical reports on IRA 2016 operation", "source_url": "SRC-001; SRC-003; SRC-004; SRC-002", "text": "SRC-001 DOJ indictment: alleges the Internet Research Agency was a Russian organization conducting election and political-process interference from around 2014; funding from Yevgeniy Prigozhin and Concord entities; false U.S. personas and stolen identities operated social-media pages/groups on divisive issues; U.S.-based infrastructure and travel under false pretenses were used to conceal Russian origin and collect intelligence. The indictment alleges a strategic goal to sow discord; by early/mid-2016 operations supported Donald Trump and disparaged Hillary Clinton; defendants bought social-media political ads, staged U.S. rallies, solicited/compensated real U.S. persons, and communicated with unwitting Trump-associated individuals. It describes the translator project on YouTube, Facebook, Instagram and Twitter, over 80 employees by July 2016, a goal to spread distrust toward candidates and the political system, and Concord as primary funder. Project Lakhta monthly budget exceeded 73 million rubles (over $1.25m) by September 2016, with funds disguised as software support and routed through about 14 affiliate bank accounts. The IRA employed hundreds, had an annual budget of millions, and included graphics, data-analysis, SEO, IT, and finance departments. SRC-003 Senate Volume II: finds IRA social-media information warfare in 2016 spread disinformation and societal division, masqueraded as Americans, used targeted advertisements, false news, self-generated content and platform tools to attempt to deceive tens of millions; sought to polarize Americans by societal, ideological and racial differences, provoke real-world events, and covertly support Russia's favored candidate. Committee says platform scope is partly unknown because it relied on companies to identify/share activity. Findings: IRA sought to harm Clinton and support Trump at Kremlin direction; broader goal to undermine faith in democratic process, denigrate Clinton and harm her electability; campaign emphasized race, immigration and Second Amendment issues; African-Americans were the most targeted group, with over 66% of Facebook ads containing race-related terms, Blacktivist generating 11.2m engagements, five of top 10 Instagram accounts focused on African-American issues, and 96% of YouTube content targeted racial issues/police brutality. Paid ads were not key: about $100,000 in Facebook ads, nearly 3,400 Facebook/Instagram ads versus 61,500 Facebook posts, 116,000 Instagram posts, and 10.4m tweets. IRA also coopted unwitting Americans offline, including petitions, personal information, self-defense courses, and rallies, some involving requests for Trump Campaign assistance. SRC-004 technical report abstract: IRA campaigns on Facebook, Instagram and Twitter reached tens of millions; over 30m users shared IRA Facebook/Instagram posts 2015-17; activity peaked around political dates/crises; began on Twitter in 2013 and evolved into multi-platform activity; organic posting was more far-reaching than ads. Report assesses activities were designed to polarize: encourage African-American voters to boycott elections or follow wrong voting procedures; encourage extreme right-wing voters to be confrontational; spread sensationalist/conspiratorial/junk news and misinformation across the spectrum. Activity continued after the 2016 election and increased. SRC-002 NPR summarizes the Senate findings: focus on African-American audiences, 66% race-related Facebook content, location targeting principally aimed at African-Americans in key metropolitan areas, Blacktivist 11.2m engagements, and post-election increases of Instagram 238%, Facebook 59%, Twitter 52%, YouTube citations 84%."}
